      "writtenOn": "2025-10-01T06:37:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I briefly tried this just now and I think it\u0027s a bit hard to do atm because of a couple issues:\n\n1) In order for the read lock to really make sense, we should be holding it whenever we use any of the object handles. This is problematic because right now, we call `ProcessEvents` on all `WGPUInstance`s at the end of command handling. If we were to do this while holding a read lock, any callbacks that get triggered would then try to acquire the write lock which would deadlock. This could potentially be solved if we had a per-`WGPU`Object lock, however...\n\n2) Similar to the above, something like calling `device.Destroy()` results in the same issue because we would need to be holding the read lock to access the `WGPUDevice`, but calling `Destroy()` could trigger the device lost callback which would then try to acquire the write lock. This wouldn\u0027t work w.r.t the per `WGPU`Object lock because it would require acquiring the device lock both times.\n\nI think in order to solve those issues, we would need something like a recursive rw-lock where a read lock could be promoted to a write lock if/when needed. I briefly searched for something like this but wasn\u0027t able to find anything convincing. That said, I am inclined to leave this like this for now, and once I update more of the server-side code, I think it might be possible to simplify the locking strategy.",
      "parentUuid": "be61b951_95884859",
      "revId": "897c19bc5c689c941c526ded60ba29ea6d39ee2f",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f3e8577d_a75ecb76",
        "filename": "generator/templates/dawn/wire/server/ServerBase.h",
